---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navigation from '../components/Navigation.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="About - Mingxi Yan">
  <Navigation />

  <main class="flex-grow">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <section class="py-20 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 bg-gradient-to-b from-gray-50 to-white">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
        <h1 class="text-5xl md:text-6xl font-bold mb-6 opacity-0 animate-fade-in-up">About Me</h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 max-w-3xl opacity-0 animate-fade-in-up delay-100">
          A journey through code, cards, and creativity.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Main Content -->
    <section class="py-16 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <!-- Introduction -->
          <div class="mb-16">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 flex items-center">
              <span class="w-12 h-1 bg-black mr-4"></span>
              Who I Am
            </h2>
            <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              <p>
                Life unfolds like a vast, uncharted horizon, where each step forward is both a choice and a surrender. 
                Freedom is not the absence of constraints, but the quiet courage to embrace the unknown, to dwell in moments that reveal themselves only when all else falls away. 
                The weight of decisions does not stifle, but gives form to the endless possibilities that beckon just beyond reach. 
                In these moments, existence feels like a dance with time—each movement, deliberate yet fleeting, filled with purpose yet rooted in uncertainty.
                To live fully is to recognize the delicate balance between the paths chosen and those left behind. The journey is not about control but about allowing oneself to be shaped by experiences, letting the world reveal its truths, sometimes softly, sometimes harshly. 
                In this openness, there is freedom. An authentic, profound sense of belonging to the world, not as a conqueror, but as a participant in its unfolding mystery.
                The beauty of life lies not in mastery, but in the gentle awareness that each day offers a chance to rediscover what it means to be, to think, to choose, and ultimately, to be free.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Four Pillars -->
          <div class="mb-16">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 flex items-center">
              <span class="w-12 h-1 bg-black mr-4"></span>
              When I'm Not Philosophizing
            </h2>

            <div class="space-y-8">
              <!-- Computer Science -->
              <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-black p-6">
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">💻 Computer Science</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                  I came to code late—only after university—which means I still remember what it feels like to not understand it. That perspective
                  keeps me grounded. There's something deeply satisfying about building systems piece by piece, watching small components combine
                  into something larger, something that actually works. When it breaks, the machine doesn't lie; it tells you exactly where your
                  thinking went wrong. That brutal honesty is liberating. The craft isn't in writing clever code—it's in creating systems where
                  each piece clicks into place, where complexity resolves into clarity. The best code doesn't just run; it reads like the clearest
                  expression of an idea, elegant in structure, inevitable in logic.
                </p>
              </div>

              <!-- Poker -->
              <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-black p-6">
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">♠ Poker</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                  Poker is where calculation meets instinct, where uncertainty and probability collide in real-time. I study GTO charts not to
                  memorize them blindly, but to understand the reasoning beneath the ranges, the logic behind the frequencies. That understanding
                  gives me the freedom to deviate because the real art isn't playing optimally, it's knowing when to bend the rules because your
                  opponent isn't optimal either. Every fold is data, every bet is a question, every river card a reminder that you can't control
                  the outcome, only the decision. The math keeps you in the game, but reading ane exploiting separates competence from mastery.
                </p>
              </div>

              <!-- Writing -->
              <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-black p-6">
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">✍️ Writing</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                  Writing is thinking made visible, the slow work of turning vague intuition into something sharp enough to cut. It's where I go
                  to express what gets buried beneath the fast pace of daily life, emotions deeply hidden in tiny moments that would otherwise slip
                  away unnoticed. I write to stop, to reflect, to capture what lives beneath the surface. Wittgenstein believed the limits of
                  language are the limits of our world, and I take that chanllenge constantly: the speaking of what exists beyond words, of
                  finding sentences that expand rather than constrain understanding. Poetry, essays, fragments, whatever form the idea demands. I
                  write to discover what I think, and sometimes, if I'm lucky, the sentence that emerges is clearer than the thought that started
                  it, when I know I'm onto something.
                </p>
              </div>

              <!-- Basketball -->
              <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-black p-6">
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">🏀 Basketball</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                  On the basketball court, there's a bit of Kyrie in my step, quick, unpredictable, finding space where none seems to exist. What
                  draws me to the game is its artistry: creative moves that tricks the camera, beautiful passes threaded through tight windows, cuts
                  and shots so precise they feel like geometry come alive. The wordless communication, eyes meeting across the court, a subtle hand
                  gesture that shifts the entire play, that's the deepest kind of teamwork. Every possession is pattern recognition at full speed,
                  anticipation and reaction flowing into one continuous motion. And yes, there's that moment when you kill the game, when the shot
                  drops and the crowd screams. That's the payoff for all the repetition refined into instinct, for making the complex look effortless.
                </p>
              </div>

              <!-- Gaming -->
              <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-black p-6">
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">🎮 Gaming</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                  I play all kinds of games, each appealing to something different: strategic games for planning and foresight, creative games for
                  building something from nothing, action games for that pure rush when the heartbeat quickens and reflexes take over. But regardless
                  of genre, my approach stays consistent: understand the essence first. Before grinding for wins, I want to know why the system works
                  the way it does, what makes it tick. That understanding becomes the booster, the thing that helps me master games quickly. The
                  victories come quietly, not from loud triumphs but from seeing the pattern emerge, from turning complexity into simplicity. Once
                  you grasp the underlying logic, the optimal play becomes obvious. No rage quits, no tilting, just the calm satisfaction of watching
                  a plan unfold exactly as predicted, or the quick pivot when it doesn't.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Philosophy -->
          <div class="mb-16">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 flex items-center">
              <span class="w-12 h-1 bg-black mr-4"></span>
              My Philosophy
            </h2>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 p-8">
              <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                <p class="text-lg italic font-serif">
                  "Be curious. Stay humble. Build things that matter."
                </p>
                <p>
                  I believe in continuous learning—not because I have to, but because I genuinely can't imagine living any other way.
                  Every skill I develop, every book I read, every conversation I have adds another tool to my mental toolkit.
                </p>
                <p>
                  I believe in systems thinking. Most problems aren't isolated; they're part of larger patterns. Whether debugging
                  code or analyzing a poker hand, the solution often lies in understanding the broader context.
                </p>
                <p>
                  I believe in balancing logic with intuition, analysis with creativity, individual excellence with collaboration.
                  The best solutions rarely come from extremes—they come from finding the right synthesis.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- What I'm Looking For -->
          <div class="mb-16">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 flex items-center">
              <span class="w-12 h-1 bg-black mr-4"></span>
              What I'm Looking For
            </h2>
            <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              <p>
                I'm looking for opportunities where I can apply my technical skills to meaningful problems. I want to work with
                teams that value both rigorous engineering and creative problem-solving. I'm drawn to projects at the intersection
                of technology and human experience—tools that genuinely make people's lives better.
              </p>
              <p>
                I'm also looking for conversations. If you're interested in technology, game theory, philosophy, or the intersection
                of any of these fields, I'd love to connect. Some of my best ideas have come from unexpected conversations with
                curious people.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Fun Facts -->
          <div class="mb-16">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 flex items-center">
              <span class="w-12 h-1 bg-black mr-4"></span>
              A Few More Things
            </h2>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600">
              <li class="flex items-start">
                <span class="mr-3 text-xl">•</span>
                <span>I think the best code is code that explains itself</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start">
                <span class="mr-3 text-xl">•</span>
                <span>I'm convinced that most complex problems can be solved with a whiteboard and enough time</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start">
                <span class="mr-3 text-xl">•</span>
                <span>I believe everyone should learn to play poker—it's a masterclass in decision theory</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start">
                <span class="mr-3 text-xl">•</span>
                <span>I spend a lot of time gaming, and I think deeply while playing—every move is a thought, every system a puzzle</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start">
                <span class="mr-3 text-xl">•</span>
                <span>Love songs are sad, but sadness is poetic—it's where feeling becomes art</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start">
                <span class="mr-3 text-xl">•</span>
                <span>Solitude gives me time to feel the world, to notice what others rush past</span>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Contact Section -->
    <section class="py-16 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 bg-black text-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-8 text-center">Let's Talk</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-8 text-center">
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-bold mb-3">Professional</h3>
            <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/mingxi-yan-0b9581272" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-gray-300 hover:text-white transition-colors">
              LinkedIn
            </a>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-bold mb-3">Code</h3>
            <a href="https://github.com/PittsIrv" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-gray-300 hover:text-white transition-colors">
              GitHub
            </a>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-bold mb-3">Direct</h3>
            <a href="mailto:mingxiy66@gmail.com" class="text-gray-300 hover:text-white transition-colors">
              Email
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-12 text-center">
          <p class="text-gray-400">
            Whether you want to discuss a project, share ideas, or just say hi—I'm always happy to connect.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .prose h2 {
    color: #000;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .prose h3 {
    color: #000;
    font-weight: 700;
  }
</style>
